e ing  okay so  we interchange i and j  so what we have is the  ji is equal to  alpha delta j i  delta k l plus beta  delta j k delta il  because we have to replace the i by the  g wherever it appears  and the j by the i wherever it appears  delta j l delta i k  all right now uh  we want to subtract these two things  these two equations  okay so let's call this as uh maybe  star one and start  two we want to do star one minus star  two  you please note that on the left hand  side of star 1 i have  c i j k l on the left hand side of star  2 i have c j i k  but c i j k l is equal to c g i k l from  the minor symmetries  okay so what we'll have on the left hand  side is  0 and here  you please note that delta i j is very  much equal to delta j  i okay so these two terms also cancel  what we have left is beta  ah now you know that this is delta i k  this is delta j  this is delta i k this is delta j so  effectively what we can do is we can  take this common  with the gamma so beta minus gamma  delta i k delta  j l and here also you note  that this delta i l is present here also  delta n  together with delta j k which is also  present here okay  so we can write this as plus  gamma minus beta delta  il delta jk  now this equation that we have just  obtained  it must be true irrespective of our  choice  of the indices now  please note the significance of this  statement suppose you were to  choose i equal to 1 k equal to  let's say let's say 3  j equal to 2 and l equal to 4  okay so this term would be 0 now i is 1  l is 4 this also becomes 0 okay  so this is identically satisfied so no  particular or fresh information is  provided to us  if we make that choice we can keep on  making these choices  okay but and  in most of them you will see that you  end up with 0 equal to 0 so no problem  no problem but no no usefulness also  but suppose we were to take something  like this  that i equal to 1 a equal to 1  and j equal to 2  l equal to  2 so let's try this suppose we take i  equal to 1  equal to 1 j equal to  um well let's take uh  equal to 2 and l equal to 2  so here also we should be doing the same  thing so i equal to 1  l equal to 2 and we don't even have to  worry about the rest of it  because whenever the two indices of the  chronicle delta are not equal  this is the entire thing is going to be  zero okay but anyway just let's write  write this down j is 2 and k is 1 so  this also is 0 okay so overall this just  goes away but this one  you note it remains and very importantly  it must be true uh this relation must be  true  as we have said irrespective of our  choice of the indices  and if that is so then the only  conclusion that we can draw from here  is that beta must be equal to gamma  okay in a similar fashion uh  if we go back to this first relation and  then  maybe we interchange  the j and l will also end up with again  this kind of a  relationship that beta is equal to gamma  so overall  as i had mentioned earlier what is  indeed happening  is that uh although these are these were  appearing as  three independent constants alpha beta  gamma but in reality  as you have seen that beta is equal to  gamma so indeed  there are two independent constants uh  as i had mentioned in the  uh in the previous lecture so let's  write this down  in using just two constants in the next  page  so c i j k l  is equal to if we take a look at the  previous page  this is alpha delta i j delta kl  alpha delta  alpha delta ij delta kl  plus beta we take  it common with the third term  plus delta il delta j k  all right now this particular  relationship  is uh what is describing the isotropic  material behavior  in addition to the to of course the  previous assumption  that is a linear behavior that it is not  accounting for any rate or history  effects and  because we are dealing with constants  here the assumption of uniformity or  homogeneity is very much embedded within  it  but very importantly we have now also  embedded within it  the in